<h1>Stacking Machines &amp; Stackers</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A stacking machine collects individual products from a conveyor and forms them into organized stacks, slugs, or layers for downstream wrapping, cartoning, <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packing</a>, or palletizing. Types include counter stackers (count and stack individual items), layer formers (arrange cases or products into pallet-layer patterns), and case stackers (stack sealed cases for palletizer infeed). The right stacking method depends on product stability, count accuracy requirements, downstream machine cadence, and how much accumulation is needed upstream.</p>
<h2 id="counter-heading">Counter Stackers</h2>
<p>Counter stackers count individual products on a high-speed conveyor belt, separate counted groups from the continuous stream, and form each group into a stack for wrapping or cartoning. The counting, separating, and stacking operations happen in sequence at the production line's full speed, any speed mismatch causes miscounts, jams, or product damage.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/stacking-forpak-servo-stacker.webp" width="800" height="533" alt="A stainless steel stacking machine with blue infeed belts, a touchscreen panel, wire mesh guards, and a row of discharge lanes separated by white dividers." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A Forpak servo stacker. The machine builds stacks and discharges them onto a continuously running take-away conveyor. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.forpak.com/servo-stackers" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Forpak</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Counting Methods</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Photoeye counting:</strong> Through-beam or retro-reflective photoeyes detect product edges. Each leading-edge triggers a count. Works for opaque products with consistent shape. Standard for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/cookie-cracker/">cookie and cracker lines</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Laser profiling:</strong> Laser sensors measure product height profile. Distinguishes overlapping products, counts products of varying size, and detects missing or broken products. Costs more than photoeyes but handles difficult products better.</li>
<li><strong>Vision counting:</strong> Camera systems count and inspect products simultaneously. Detect orientation, color, shape defects, and count in a single operation. Usually the most expensive option and the most capable for complex products.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Stacking Formats</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vertical stacking:</strong> Products stack on top of each other in a vertical pile. Standard for cookies, crackers, napkins, and sheets. Stack height = count × product thickness.</li>
<li><strong>On-edge (slug) stacking:</strong> Products stand on edge in a horizontal row, forming a slug. Standard for sandwich cookies, wafers, and products packaged in sleeve format. See the <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/cookie-cracker/">cookie packaging guide</a> for slug-wrap details.</li>
<li><strong>Shingled stacking:</strong> Products overlap in a cascading pattern (like roof shingles). Used when products are too fragile to stack directly or when the packaging format requires overlapping presentation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Published Speed Examples</h3>
<p>Counter stacker speed depends on product size, fragility, stack count, and lane count, so published figures vary widely:</p>
<ul>
<li>Forpak rates its <a href="https://www.forpak.com/servo-stackers" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GSS servo stacker</a> at 100 pieces per minute per lane, built with 1 to 10 lanes, for frozen meat patties and bakery products.</li>
<li>Syntegon lists equipment for <a href="https://www.syntegon.com/solutions/food/cookies-packaging-machine/on-edge-cookies-packaging-machine/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">on-edge cookie and cracker packaging lines</a> with a count feeder handling up to 3,600 cookies per minute and measuring loaders feeding flow wrappers at up to 400 slug packs per minute.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ask suppliers for a rated speed with your product, count, and stack format, and for the count accuracy they will accept at that speed.</p>
<h2 id="layer-heading">Layer Formers</h2>
<p>Layer formers arrange individual cases, bundles, or bags into a complete pallet layer pattern before palletizing. The layer former is the bridge between the packaging line (producing individual units in single file) and the palletizer (stacking complete layers onto the pallet).</p>
<p>Layer forming methods:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Row-forming and sweeping:</strong> Cases accumulate into a row on a conveyor. When the row is complete, a sweep mechanism (pusher bar) pushes the entire row onto a layer-building table. Multiple rows build the complete layer pattern. The full layer then transfers to the palletizer. This is the most common method for conventional palletizing.</li>
<li><strong>Robotic layer forming:</strong> An articulated arm or gantry robot picks individual cases and places them into the layer pattern on a staging conveyor. Handles any case orientation (flat, on-side, on-end) and complex patterns. Recipe-based changeover for different layer patterns. Usually costs more than row forming but offers the most pattern flexibility.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/conveyor-functions/product-handling/">Turn and orient</a> layer forming:</strong> Cases travel on a conveyor through turning stations that rotate selected cases 90 degrees to create interlocking patterns. Alternating row orientations create a stable, interlocked pallet load that resists shifting during transport.</li>
</ul>
<p>Layer former speed must match or exceed the case packer output. A 25 CPM case packer with 5 cases per row and 5 rows per layer forms one complete layer every 60 seconds. The layer former and palletizer must cycle within that time. Layer forming speed varies by palletizer class. Columbia lists its <a href="https://palletizing.com/floor-level-palletizers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FLD6200 floor-level palletizer</a> at up to 100 cases per minute and its <a href="https://palletizing.com/solutions/inline-palletizers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HL9200 inline palletizer</a> at up to 200 cases per minute, depending on pattern.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/stacking-columbia-fld2500-palletizer.webp" width="900" height="471" alt="A floor level palletizer in a grey enclosure, fed by a curved roller conveyor carrying a line of cardboard cases." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A Columbia FLD2500 floor level palletizer with its case infeed conveyor. Cases arrive in single file and are formed into layers inside the machine. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://palletizing.com/floor-level-palletizers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Columbia Machine</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="case-heading">Case Stackers</h2>
<p>Case stackers receive sealed cases from a <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case sealer conveyor</a> and stack them vertically in small groups for downstream handling. Stacked cases take less conveyor space and can be picked as a group by palletizer grippers, improving palletizer cycle efficiency.</p>
<p>Case stackers use a lift-and-place or drop mechanism. The first case stops on a platform. The next case arrives, and the mechanism lifts the first case and places the second case underneath (bottom stacking) or stacks the second case on top (top stacking). Top stacking risks crushing lower cases if case strength is marginal. Bottom stacking preserves case integrity by never placing weight on sealed product.</p>
<p>Case weight and rate drive the lift mechanism choice: pneumatic for lighter, simpler duty, and servo, electric, or hydraulic drives for heavier cases or tighter positioning. Case stacker speed must match upstream output. As one example, Knight Industrial lists its dairy <a href="https://www.knightindustrialinc.com/machines/case-stacker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">case stacker</a> at 18 to 35 cases per minute depending on configuration, in electric, air, or hydraulic versions. <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/pallet-dispenser/">Pallet dispensers</a> feed empty pallets to the palletizer downstream.</p>
<h2 id="integration-heading">Conveyor Integration</h2>
<p>Stacking machines integrate with upstream and downstream conveyors through speed synchronization, sensor coordination, and PLC communication. Key integration points:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Infeed conveyor:</strong> Delivers products at controlled spacing. <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/">Infeed systems</a> use timing screws, speed-differential belts, or servo phasing to establish the correct product pitch for the stacker's counting cycle.</li>
<li><strong>Outfeed conveyor:</strong> Carries completed stacks to the next operation (wrapper, cartoner, case packer). Outfeed speed must match the stacker's output rate to prevent stack accumulation or starvation.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation</a>:</strong> Buffer conveyors between the stacker and downstream equipment absorb speed mismatches. Critical when the wrapper or case packer stops for film splices, jam clearing, or changeover.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">Reject conveyor</a>:</strong> Removes miscounted stacks, broken products, or out-of-spec groups before they reach the wrapper. Vision or weight verification triggers the reject.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a complete view of conveyor automation and controls architecture, see the <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems guide</a>. For cost considerations, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
