<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a stacking machine?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A stacking machine collects individual products from a conveyor and forms organized stacks, slugs, or layers for wrapping, <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packing</a>, or palletizing. Types include counter stackers, layer formers, and case stackers.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a counter stacker?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Counts individual products on a high-speed belt, groups them into a set count, and stacks them vertically or on-edge. Speed depends on product and machine: Forpak rates its GSS at 100 pieces per minute per lane, and Syntegon lists cookie count feeders at up to 3,600 cookies per minute. Common in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/cookie-cracker/">cookie/cracker</a>, printing, and tissue packaging.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Stacker vs. palletizer?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A stacker forms product groups (stacks, slugs, layers). A <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">palletizer</a> places those groups onto a pallet. The stacker operates upstream, it feeds the palletizer.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What industries use stacking machines?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Bakery, printing, tissue/napkin, corrugated, pharmaceutical, and end-of-line packaging. Any operation needing counted, organized groups from a continuous product stream.</p>

</div>
